=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The fault lives in router6's nftables FORWARD chain, on its `eth2` interface — the physical link to router3 (`r6_r3`). Three injected rules block every packet that arrives on that interface: one drops all TCP, one drops all UDP, and a final catch-all drops anything else, including ICMP. A single narrow ACCEPT rule above them permits only TCP/80 from the 172.16.10.0/24 subnet destined to 172.18.51.1; everything else is silently discarded. The drop counter already recorded 24 packets (2016 bytes) actively blocked.

Because router6 is the sole upstream gateway for mswitch2's segment, all hosts behind it (pc15, pc16, pc17, pc18 on switch10) rely on return traffic flowing back through router3 → router6 eth2. With that path completely blocked, ping commands on those hosts hang indefinitely waiting for replies that never arrive, which caused the exec_shell 10-second timeout and the `status=unknown` rows seen in the reachability map.

The user's claim that laptop2 and laptop3 cannot communicate is not corroborated by any evidence: a direct L2 ping between them (172.16.10.12 → 172.16.10.13) returns 0% loss at sub-millisecond latency. Switch1 and its ports are fully operational; the fault is entirely on router6 in the mswitch2/router6 segment.

Root cause: nftables FORWARD chain rules on router6 block all inbound traffic on `eth2`, severing the return path for the pc15–pc18 segment.

Fix: Remove the three drop rules from router6's `ip filter FORWARD` chain — specifically the `iifname "eth2" tcp drop`, `iifname "eth2" udp drop`, and `iifname "eth2" drop` entries — using `nft delete rule ip filter FORWARD handle <N>` for each, restoring normal forwarding from router3 into the mswitch2 segment.
=== END ===